 "drivingQuestion": "Why must the Tosefta state that a convert's wife drinks?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "converts' wives drink by the amplification 'and say'",
 "continuesQuestionOf": "sotah.sugya156",
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sotah.26a.11",
   "daf": "26a",
   "sentenceId": 10,
   "he": "מַהוּ דְּתֵימָא: ״דַּבֵּר אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, וְלֹא גֵּרִים, קָא מַשְׁמַע לַן. וְאֵימָא הָכִי נָמֵי! ״וְאָמַרְתָּ״ — רִבּוּיָא הוּא.",
   "en": "The Gemara answers: It is necessary «lest you say» that she does not drink, as the verse states: «“Speak to the children of Israel,» and say unto them: If the wife of any man goes astray, and acts unfaithfully against him” (Numbers 5:12). One might have inferred from this verse that the sota ritual applies only to those born as Jews «and not» to «converts;» the baraita in the Tosefta therefore «teaches us» that this is not so. The Gemara asks: Why not «say» that «indeed» the verse excludes converts? The Gemara answers: The subsequent term: «“And say» unto them” (Numbers 5:12) «is an amplification,» which serves to include converts."
  }
 ],
